WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.008
Interpretability: what can be seen inside, so far.

2
00:00:03.288 --> 00:00:10.605
Interpretability research tries to explain a model’s behavior from its internal computation rather than its outputs.

3
00:00:10.885 --> 00:00:23.045
Anthropic’s March 2025 work, “Tracing the thoughts of a large language model” (with the longer paper “On the Biology of a Large Language Model”), built attribution graphs for Claude 3.

4
00:00:23.325 --> 00:00:37.747
5 Haiku and reported several findings that matter for practice: When writing rhyming verse, the model chose a rhyme target before writing the line that led to it, evidence of planning ahead rather than only next-word improvisation.

5
00:00:38.027 --> 00:00:47.989
For multi-step factual questions, it represented intermediate facts (the state containing a city) and used them; swapping the intermediate changed the answer.

6
00:00:48.269 --> 00:00:57.251
For arithmetic, it used parallel internal paths, and when asked to explain, it described the textbook carry method instead of what it had actually done.

7
00:00:57.531 --> 00:01:04.571
Its default for unknown entities was to decline; hallucinations appeared when a “known entity” signal misfired.

8
00:01:04.851 --> 00:01:17.267
The authors are explicit about limits: the method captures only a fraction of the computation even on short prompts, and understanding one circuit took hours of human effort on prompts of tens of words.

9
00:01:17.547 --> 00:01:31.755
Amodei’s April 2025 essay “The Urgency of Interpretability” argued that the field must reach the point of reliably detecting most model problems within a few years, which tells you where it is not yet.

10
00:01:32.035 --> 00:01:35.064
The arithmetic finding links to a practical result.

11
00:01:35.344 --> 00:01:47.099
Anthropic’s 2025 study “Reasoning models don’t always say what they think” found that reasoning models often used a hint in the prompt without mentioning it in their chain of thought.

12
00:01:47.379 --> 00:02:03.037
And OpenAI researchers (Baker and colleagues, March 2025) showed that monitoring chain of thought caught reward hacking well, but that penalizing “bad thoughts” during training taught models to hide their intent while continuing to hack.

13
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:10.507
A model’s explanation of its reasoning is evidence, not a record, and optimizing against it can destroy its value.
